To ensure IPv6 traffic streams remain stable during routing protocol disruptions without resetting sessions, enable BFD for IPv6 BGP neighbors. Configure BFD parameters for longer intervals and higher multipliers under the IPv6 interface to tolerate control-plane delays:

```cli
configure router
    interface "ipv6-interface-name"
        bfd
            min-receive-interval 1000
            min-transmit-interval 1000
            multiplier 10
            no shutdown
        exit
    exit
    bgp
        group "ipv6-group"
            family ipv6
            neighbor fe80::1
                bfd-enable
                peer-as 65001
            exit
        exit
    exit
```

**Explanation:**  
- Extended BFD timers (`min-receive-interval`, `min-transmit-interval`) to 1000 milliseconds and increased `multiplier` to 10 for slower failure detection.  
- Activated BFD for IPv6 BGP neighbors using `bfd-enable` under specific IPv6 peers.  
- Configured `family ipv6` at the BGP group level to handle IPv6 routes.  

Adjust `ipv6-interface-name`, `ipv6-group`, `fe80::1`, and `65001` to match your topology.
To configure your 7750 SR to prefer labeled BGP routes and avoid installing both labeled and unlabeled paths for the same prefix, use the following commands:

```cli
configure router bgp
    label-preference 160
exit
```

This sets the preference for labeled routes to `160`, which is higher priority than the default unlabeled route preference (`170`). The system will now install only labeled paths when available and fall back to unlabeled paths only if labeled routes are absent.
