To configure BGP on the Nokia 7750 SR to treat AS 65001 as the parent AS while internally grouping AS 65100 and AS 65200 as sub-ASes, use the `local-as` command with the `private` and `no-prepend-global-as` options. This setup hides the sub-AS numbers externally and presents them as a single AS 65001.

### Configuration for a router in AS 65100:
```cli
configure router bgp
    autonomous-system 65001
    group "INTERNAL_GROUP"
        peer-as 65001
        local-as 65100 private no-prepend-global-as
        neighbor <neighbor-IP>
            exit
        exit
    exit
```

### Configuration for a router in AS 65200:
```cli
configure router bgp
    autonomous-system 65001
    group "INTERNAL_GROUP"
        peer-as 65001
        local-as 65200 private no-prepend-global-as
        neighbor <neighbor-IP>
            exit
        exit
    exit
```

### Key Points:
1. **`autonomous-system 65001`**: Sets the global AS to the parent AS (65001) for external peering.
2. **`peer-as 65001`**: Specifies that peers in the group belong to the parent AS.
3. **`local-as <sub-AS>`**: Assigns the internal sub-AS (65100 or 65200) for the group.
4. **`private`**: Hides the sub-AS in paths learned from peers.
5. **`no-prepend-global-as`**: Prevents prepending the global AS (65001) when advertising routes to peers in this group.

Replace `<neighbor-IP>` with the actual IP addresses of BGP peers in the parent AS. This configuration ensures that external peers see only AS 65001, while internal sub-ASes (65100/65200) operate as distinct entities within the parent AS.
