To set BGP routes from a specific neighbor as a backup by adjusting their preference, use the following CLI commands. Replace `<group-name>` and `<neighbor-ip>` with your actual BGP group and neighbor IP:

```cli
configure router bgp
    group "<group-name>"
        neighbor <neighbor-ip>
            preference 200
        exit
    exit
exit
```

**Explanation:**  
- The `preference` command sets the administrative distance for routes learned from this neighbor.  
- A higher value (e.g., `200`) makes these routes **less preferred** compared to other protocols (default BGP preference is `170`).  
- This ensures the routes act as a backup, only used if better options (lower preference) are unavailable.  

> **Note:** If you meant to adjust BGP path selection *within* the AS (e.g., making routes less preferred than other BGP paths), use `local-preference` with a **lower** value (default is `100`). Example:  
> ```cli
> configure router bgp
>     group "<group-name>"
>         neighbor <neighbor-ip>
>             local-preference 50
>         exit
>     exit
> exit
> ```
